+# -- the exposed under-robe column ---------------------------------------------
+# Below the raked hem the under-robe stops being a lining held 2 mm off the silk
+# and becomes a surface in its own right, seen in full for a third of a metre.
+# The three constants here are the answer to the sharpest note any panel has
+# given this design, delivered 3/3 and about the very surface the previous panel
+# had ordered built: "a pale ribbed barrel or tree stump the robe has been hiked
+# up onto", "a squat flat-sided drum", "nearly as wide as the robe base".
+#
+# The mistake was treating "exposed" as a MATERIAL problem. It is a silhouette
+# problem first. A lining follows the garment's own profile, and the garment's
+# profile at the hem is a FLARE - cloth that has hit the floor and spread - so
+# inheriting it gave the column the widest radius on the model at the one height
+# where it is fully visible. That inverts the taper: the silhouette goes narrow
+# at the waist, wide at the hem, narrower at the cut, then wide again at the
+# floor. Nothing about the value would have fixed that.
